ironment narrows but does not erase the Republican advantage", "Polymarket prices a Democratic win at roughly 17%"], "rationale": "The district's Republican lean, Hamadeh's six-to-one money advantage and a rating consensus of Likely-to-Solid Republican outweigh a favorable Democratic climate, pointing to a comfortable but not safe Republican hold. With no polls available, uncertainty remains wide.", "watch": "Late district polling or Democratic spending showing Greene-Placentia within single digits would move the race toward tossup territory.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings.", "Incumbent Abraham Hamadeh (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Arizona's 8th District leans Republican by about eight points, and first-term incumbent Abraham Hamadeh is seeking re-election with the advantages that come with it: name recognition from a high-profile tenure, a roughly six-to-one fundraising edge, and no reported outside spending against him. Challenger Bernadette Greene-Placentia, a former trucker who won the Democratic nomination, has raised about $209,000 and remains lightly funded.\n\nMost expert raters place the race between Likely and Solid Republican, and prediction markets give the Democrat roughly a one-in-six chance. No public general-election polls are available, so the outlook rests on district fundamentals, which show a favorable national climate for Democrats trimming but not erasing the Republican edge. Hamadeh is a clear favorite, though the absence of polling keeps uncertainty wider than in a well-surveyed race.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.0839, "margin": -8.56}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.0923, "margin": -9.5}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.0824, "margin": -10.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.1327, "margin": -8.0}]}